{:edb/db-before-t 2591 :edb/db-after-t 2592 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535904 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:19:53.869Z" 13194139535904 true] [17592186133520 1100 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071876/assets/16f06043e1f8bcab11a7e43fc76800c33e2560bb00e425c9c88f8cfeb8c12ee3.png)" 13194139535904 true] [17592186133520 1100 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535904 false] [17592186133520 1145 "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nIn our case, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (14)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 false] [17592186133520 1145 "Recall that the matrix $P_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T$ maps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071876/assets/fde70db50cff408347bdebc086fbfa3ae87020bb0300b32dad67168d617ed607.png) Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (14)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 true] [17592186133521 1100 "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071876/assets/5b3093e1c65624c862d949706ed5922ab8078beac752d4e7347c71be993580d8.png)" 13194139535904 true] [17592186133521 1100 "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535904 false] [17592186133521 1145 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if $Q^2=Q$ and $Q^T=Q$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$A^T = A$\nbut\n$A^{2}|= A \\cdot A; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne A$.\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$B^{T} \\ne B$.\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n$C^T = C$\nand\n$C^{2}|= C \\cdot C; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = C$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139535904 false] [17592186133521 1145 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if $Q^2=Q$ and $Q^T=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$A^T = A$\nbut \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{T}\\ne B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n$C^T = C$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139535904 true] [17592186133522 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071876/assets/21268cd488766dc03da672cb931f1678644d2ada5629c90a591058d6f5954347.png)\nGiven that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ above spans $S^\\perp,$ and that $P$ 