 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(14)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 true] [17592186134179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535904 true] [17592186134179 1100 "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{22}+{p}_{31}?$" 13194139535904 true] [17592186134179 1100 "$a = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}p_{11} & p_{12} & p_{13} \\\\ p_{21} & p 22 & p_{23} \\\\ p 31 & p_{32} & p_{33}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans $S^{⊥}$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $R^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{22} + p_{31}$?" 13194139535904 false] [17592186134179 1145 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}{a}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\